Q: how to deploy the next js 13 app on iis server 10 A: <p><strong>Configure IIS:</strong></p>
<p>Open the IIS Manager on your server.</p>
<p>Create a new site or select an existing site.</p>
<p>In the site settings,
navigate to &quot;Handler Mappings.&quot;</p>
<p>Add a new module mapping with the following settings:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Request path: *</p>
</li>
<li><p>Module: iisnode</p>
</li>
<li><p>Executable: C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe</p>
</li>
<li><p>Arguments: %iisnode.dll%</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Deploy Next.js App:</strong></p>
<p>Copy the contents of the Next.js app's out directory (created during the build) to the directory where your IIS site is configured to serve files.
Configure web.config:</p>
<p>Create a web.config file in the root of your Next.js app (next to your package.json). This file contains IIS configuration settings.</p>
<p><strong>Sample web.config:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;configuration&gt;
&lt;system.webServer&gt;
&lt;handlers&gt;
  &lt;add name=&quot;iisnode&quot; path=&quot;index.js&quot; verb=&quot;*&quot; modules=&quot;iisnode&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/handlers&gt;
&lt;rewrite&gt;
  &lt;rules&gt;
    &lt;rule name=&quot;nodejs&quot;&gt;
      &lt;match url=&quot;/*&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;action type=&quot;Rewrite&quot; url=&quot;index.js&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/rule&gt;
  &lt;/rules&gt;
&lt;/rewrite&gt;
&lt;iisnode loggingEnabled=&quot;true&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/system.webServer&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Compiling Mixed C/C++ Code With C Code Using GMP Library A: <p>The problem here is that have taken a 3rd party header <code>&lt;gmp.h&gt;</code> and wrapped it with <code>extern &quot;C&quot;</code>. This happens in two places. In point.h</p>
<pre><code>#ifdef __cplusplus
extern &quot;C&quot; {
#endif

#include &lt;gmp.h&gt;

...

#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif

</code></pre>
<p>and in main.cpp when you include point.h</p>
<pre><code>
extern &quot;C&quot; {
#include &quot;point.h&quot;
}

</code></pre>
<p>In general this is a bad idea, and in this specific case what seems to be happening is that gmp.h, when compiled as C++, is including a C++ header file <code>&lt;iosfwd&gt;</code>. Since this has been wrapped in <code>extern &quot;C&quot;</code> it is not compiling.</p>
<p>You should trust 3rd party libraries to do the right thing with regard to C/C++ interoperability. Only if you are sure that some 3rd party header is not correct should you attempt to fix it by adding <code>extern &quot;C&quot;</code>. Of course <code>&lt;gmp.h&gt;</code> is a well established and widely distributed header file. I'm sure they are doing the right thing already.</p>
